### $ swytchcode --help
Execution authority for agentic API providers.

This command is also available as the shorter alias 'swy' (for example: swy list, swy exec).

Usage:
  swytchcode [command]

Project setup:
  add         Add a method, workflow, or provider
  bootstrap   Fetch all providers declared in tooling.json
  get         Fetch Wrekenfile bundles for a provider
  init        Initialize Swytchcode in this project
  policy      Manage method-level guard policies
  sync        Sync workflows and methods from the backend for installed providers
  workflow    Create, validate, publish, and pull SWF v1 workflows

Discover:
  discover    Find API capabilities matching a natural language intent
  info        Show information about a tool by canonical ID
  list        List locally available tools and providers
  plan        Show the steps for a workflow
  search      Search providers and published workflows

Run:
  demo        Explore and run demo providers
  examples    Download a working example project
  exec        Execute a tool via the Swytchcode kernel

Account:
  auth        Connect and manage third-party provider credentials
  login       Log in to your Swytchcode account
  logout      Log out and remove the local session
  whoami      Show the current authentication state
  workspace   Show or change which workspace this project uses

System:
  audit       View local execution audit log, policy violations, network activity, and stats
  config      Manage Swytchcode CLI configuration
  doctor      Run local diagnostics for the Swytchcode project
  mcp         MCP server commands

Additional Commands:
  help        Help about any command

Flags:
  -h, --help      help for swytchcode
  -v, --version   version for swytchcode

Use "swytchcode [command] --help" for more information about a command.
[exit 0]
### $ swytchcode exec --help
Execute a tool (method or workflow) via the Swytchcode kernel.

Supports two input modes:
  1. CLI args: swytchcode exec api.cluster.create --body file.json
  2. JSON stdin: echo '{"tool":"api.cluster.create","args":{...}}' | swytchcode exec

The kernel is pure, deterministic, non-interactive, and offline-capable.
It reads only local files (tooling.json, provider bundles) and never calls the registry.

Usage:
  swytchcode exec [canonical_id] [flags]

Flags:
      --allow-raw             allow execution of raw methods (required for tools starting with 'raw.')
      --body string           request body: file path or inline JSON (e.g. --body request.json or --body '{"amount":5000}')
      --demo                  run in demo mode - no project setup or API keys required
      --dry-run               show what would be executed without making the HTTP call
      --explain               show what would be executed without making any API call, then exit 0
      --header stringArray    request header key=value pairs (can be specified multiple times)
  -h, --help                  help for exec
      --input stringArray     input key=value pairs (can be specified multiple times)
      --json                  output response as JSON (single JSON object to stdout)
      --output string         write binary response body to this file (required for non-JSON/text responses)
      --param stringArray     query parameter key=value pairs (can be specified multiple times)
      --raw                   output raw HTTP response instead of normalized JSON
      --tenant string         run the call for one end user of your app, with their own connected account (your id for them)
      --tenant-label string   how approvers see that end user, e.g. "Alice Smith (alice@acme.com)" (with --tenant)
      --verbose               log request and response details to stderr for debugging (headers are redacted)
      --verbose-network       log a one-line summary of each outbound network call to stderr
[exit 0]
### $ swytchcode init --help
Initialize Swytchcode in this project

Usage:
  swytchcode init [flags]

Flags:
      --editor string     cursor | claude | codex | copilot | gemini | hermes | openclaw | windsurf | none, or with --operator: hermes | openclaw | zeroclaw | none
  -h, --help              help for init
      --mode string       production | sandbox
      --non-interactive   disable prompts; suitable for CI
      --operator          set the project up for a personal agent driven from a chat (hermes, openclaw, zeroclaw or none)
      --project string    initialise this folder (created if missing) instead of the current directory
[exit 0]
