### $ swytchcode add --help
Add tools to your project:

  swytchcode add method <canonical_id>     Enable a method from local wrekenfiles
  swytchcode add workflow <canonical_id>   Install a workflow (auto-fetches missing providers)
  swytchcode add provider <spec>           Register a provider version in tooling.json

As a shorthand, "swytchcode add <canonical_id>" enables a method directly,
equivalent to "swytchcode add method <canonical_id>".

Usage:
  swytchcode add <subcommand> [flags]
  swytchcode add [command]

Available Commands:
  method      Enable a method in tooling.json from local wrekenfiles
  provider    Add a provider version to tooling.json (explicit, reviewable; does not fetch)
  workflow    Install a workflow and auto-fetch missing providers

Flags:
  -h, --help   help for add

Use "swytchcode add [command] --help" for more information about a command.
[exit 0]
### $ swytchcode info --help
Show information about a tool by canonical ID

Usage:
  swytchcode info <canonical_id> [flags]

Flags:
  -h, --help   help for info
      --json   output as JSON
[exit 0]
### $ swytchcode list --help
Scans .swytchcode/integrations for methods and .swytchcode/workflows/ for installed workflow YAML files. Use 'tooling' to list only what has been explicitly enabled via 'swytchcode add method' or 'swytchcode add workflow'. Optional pattern filters by canonical_id or provider name (case-insensitive). No registry calls.

Usage:
  swytchcode list [methods|workflows|providers|tooling] [pattern] [flags]

Flags:
  -h, --help   help for list
      --json   output as JSON object
[exit 0]
### $ swytchcode search --help
Searches the remote registry for providers and published workflows.

Without a keyword, lists all available providers.
With a keyword, returns matching providers and published workflows via semantic search.

Results are cached for 1 hour - use --refresh to force a fresh fetch.

Usage:
  swytchcode search [intent] [flags]

Flags:
      --all         show all provider results
  -h, --help        help for search
      --json        output providers as JSON array
      --limit int   maximum provider results to show (default 10)
      --refresh     bypass cache and fetch fresh results
[exit 0]
### $ swytchcode audit --help
View local execution data stored in ~/.swytchcode/audit/: outbound network
activity, policy-violation history, and execution stats.

Run with no arguments in an interactive terminal to be prompted for which one
to view, or go straight to a subcommand:

  swytchcode audit network  - view recent outbound network calls
  swytchcode audit policy   - view recent policy-violation log entries
  swytchcode audit stats    - execution history and success rate
  swytchcode audit clear    - delete all local audit files

Note: to manage guard-policy rules (add/list/remove/validate), use the
separate 'swytchcode policy' command - that's configuration, not a log.

Usage:
  swytchcode audit [flags]
  swytchcode audit [command]

Available Commands:
  clear        Delete all local audit files
  network      Show recent outbound network calls recorded during exec
  policy       View recent policy decisions (blocked and approval requests) with status
  stats        Show local execution history and success rate

Flags:
  -h, --help            help for audit
      --tenant string   show only entries for one end user of your app (your id for them)

Use "swytchcode audit [command] --help" for more information about a command.
[exit 0]
